      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 The Authors. Journal of Advanced Nursing © 2011 Blackwell Publishing Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIM",
            "content": "The aim of this study was to assess the effectiveness of a self-management programme among osteoarthritis knee sufferers."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "Osteoarthritis of the knee is a major cause of loss of function and is common in the older people. A self-management programme is an effective way to increase arthritis self-efficacy, and helps with mastering self-management practices."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "This study was carried out from January 2008 to December 2008 and included 205 participants, with 114 in the intervention group and 91 in the control group. The 4-week trial was followed by an 8-week follow-up assessment. The outcome measures included assessments of arthritis self-efficacy, pain beliefs, unplanned medical consultations, pain days and disability days using the Mann-Whitney U-test and Friedman test."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "FINDINGS",
            "content": "At post-intervention, significant differences were found in pain beliefs (P<0·001) and pain days (P<0·004). At 8-week follow-up, there was a significant improvement in arthritis self-efficacy: pain and other symptoms subscales (P<0·001 and 0·049, respectively), pain beliefs (P<0·001) and number of unplanned medical consultations (P<0·010)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "The study supports the hypothesis that a self-management programme enhances arthritis-related self-efficacy and pain beliefs. A decrease in the number of unplanned medical consultations, pain days and disability days suggests that the programme might help reduce healthcare costs. This programme has introduced a significant result about self-efficacy and self-management to a chronic disease population in Taiwan. In short, there were great beneficial effects on self-management among persons who are affected by knee osteoarthritis."
          }
        ]
      },
